From 9d0b8af7c8fbf02d6b1a317154479631ebe9a17a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 17:33:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 27/30] kernel: add an ELF64 loader for hosts without
 linux-loader

Every libkrun host loads a caller-supplied vmlinux with linux-loader.
That crate declares its vm-memory dependency with default features, and
vm-memory's default `rawfd` feature is a `compile_error!` on Windows
targets, so a Windows build cannot contain linux-loader at all -- Cargo
unifies features across the whole graph. Windows therefore has no way to
load an external kernel.

Add the one piece of linux-loader that libkrun's ELF path actually
exercises: copy the PT_LOAD segments to the physical addresses the image
asks for and report e_entry. Nothing else in `Elf::load` is used --
kernel_offset is always None here, so the loader always places segments
at p_paddr, and the returned setup_header and kernel_end are dropped by
both callers.

Two deliberate differences from linux-loader:

  * Every segment is checked against guest memory before it is copied,
    and so is e_entry. linux-loader lets an out-of-range segment fail
    late inside the memory write and does not look at e_entry at all
    when highmem_start_address is None. On a kernel loader a bad address
    surfaces as a triple fault with no diagnostic, so it is worth naming
    the segment that does not fit.

  * The PVH ELF note is not parsed. The caller decides which boot
    protocol to use, and the only caller that needs this loader cannot
    use PVH.

The gap between p_filesz and p_memsz is left alone rather than zeroed,
exactly as linux-loader leaves it: every GuestMemoryMmap backend hands
out zeroed pages (mmap(MAP_ANONYMOUS) on Unix, VirtualAlloc with
MEM_COMMIT on Windows), and zeroing here would risk erasing a segment
copied earlier if two ever overlapped -- a real vmlinux has a trailing
bss segment whose p_offset points back into the text segment.

The module is built on every platform, not just the one that needs it,
so that the Linux test suite covers it. The tests load hand-built images
with both this loader and linux-loader and require identical guest
memory and identical entry points; linux-loader is a dev-dependency
only, and only on the platforms that can build it.

